he myth of silva being a supernatural  slayer first emerge  whereas the trope of silva being a  monster killer is a relatively recent  phenomenon within pop culture the belief  in silva's mystical properties extends  far back into human history in ancient  times silver was seen as a very pure  metal with the ancient greeks romans  macedonians phoenicians and ancient  chinese using it to help purify their  food and water ancient greeks and romans  found that water stored in silver  pitches would stay purer for longer than  water kept in wooden or clay vessels and  silver coins were even placed in  containers of water and wine to keep  liquids fresh on long ocean voyages  silver was also widely used as a  medicinal tool within these ancient  civilizations as a substance that was  used to heal wounds and eliminate  bacteria and even the father of western  medicine hippocrates referenced the  healing properties of silver in 400 bc  this is because types of silver  naturally have antimicrobial properties  which makes a useful chemical across a  wide range of medicinal purposes some of  which are still being utilized today the  point i'm making is that silver even  from very early on in human history was  seen as a metal that could heal purify  and protect when you look at it from  that perspective it's easy to consider  why the silver as a metal could be used  to take on beasts and monsters  its use as a monster-slaying weapon  however emerged much later with a  handful of varying accounts appearing  across european folklore in 19th century  germany there were stories of hunters  fashioning silver bullets to dispatch  fearsome werewolves even the brothers  grimm tapped into the myth using the  concept in their 1812 fairy tale the two  brothers in this story a character's  three silver buttons are used as bullets  in order to shoot a witch but perhaps  the first known instance of silver  emerging as a weapon against evil was in  the 1700s with the infamous account of  the beast of gevowden a real-life tale  of man vs nature that saw one of the  earliest accounts of silver bullets  being used a topic a certain keeper of  firearms and artillery knows quite a bit  about the history of silver bullets for  shooting supernatural creatures or  entities  is probably very old it might be as old  as the gun itself the first firm  reference that that i've seen and i  certainly didn't discover it as a famous  incident was the case of the beast of  gevauda in france in the 1760s and it  was thought to be this giant beast if  you've ever seen the film brotherhood of  the wolf that's a  very sort of comic book take on this i  say real incident i mean it's thought to  have been maybe a pack of particularly  wild wolves if that makes sense like  starving wolves or something like that  and more than one creature but the myth  that developed was that it was one giant  killer wolf that was just terrorizing  the countryside killing all and sundry  and various people stepped forward sort  of slayer style to come in and kill this  thing and they actually tried different  bullets out on it so lead i believe tin  as well  and silver and it's not like still they  hit it it's not like a modern horror  movie where they discover silver works  and that's it i think they just  struggled to to pin this thing down but  nonetheless they did try silver which  shows that this was a known  substance to use  while the beast of gavowowdon was a real  account and was essentially a group of  human hunters shooting wolves with  whatever they could get their hands on  there is no denying the likelihood that  it inspired european folklore  surrounding werewolves folklore  surrounding lycanthropy and werewolves  was nothing new at the time of the beast  of gavowowdon but it was a constantly  evolving subject during that period  thanks mainly due to a slate of werewolf  trials that were held across europe  between the 15th and 18th centuries  naturally as werewolf fiction began to  take hold in the 19th and 20th centuries  this idea that werewolves held a  vulnerability to silva started to become  embraced more and more as part of the  fictional law this would go on to  further take hold in the world of cinema  with the concept of silver being a  werewolf killer as first seen in the  universal classic horror 1941's the wolf  man where the titular werewolf is beaten  to death using a silver-topped walking  cane  since its first cinematic appearance  silver bullets and the idea that silva  was a monster killer exploded thanks to  popular culture particularly through  werewolf movies like silver bullets the  howling and the leathercloud 2003  vampire vs werewolf series underworld  and not to be left out of this monster  mash vampires are also supposedly  susceptible to silver sunlight garlic  and crosses have been staples of vampire  killer fiction for hundreds of years but  the hammer horror movies from the 50s  60s and 70s started mixing in silver and  specifically silver bullets as a weapon  against the iconic dracula bullish man  a bullet cannot harm me  a silver bullet  dracula  blurring the lines between fiction and  reality even further the royal armories  actually have a vampire hunter's kit  featuring a pistol and a pair of silver  bullets although the set was later  discovered to be a prop replica likely  made as a result of the popularity of  the hammer horror movies the kit serves  as another reminder to the enduring myth  that silva is a successful weapon  against monsters a myth that really took  flight across the 20th century it takes  silver to kill a werewolf  and we were taking no chances  so now we've tracked the myth of  silver's power across the centuries from  folklore origins to our current pop  culture what does that tell us about the  witches and their silver swords  well naturally being set within a world  of dark medieval fantasy the battles are  mostly fought with bows and blades so  while it's not a silver bullet geralt's  silver sword still has the same power  legacy and recognition as a monster  killer the issue here is that silver is  an incredibly soft malleable metal a  metal that isn't fit to serve as a  cutting blade even one reinforced with a  steel core  using a silver saw would be about as  much good as trying to fight someone  with a stick of licorice the only useful  thing about it is it's so soft that you  could roll it up and put it in your  backpack when you were finished so it  would be hopeless as a as a fighting  weapon but of course he's not using it  to fight humans or monsters or people in  armor or anything in in the books you  know that is his his magic sword it's  his steel sword that he uses for the the  really serious work silva being a much  softer metal than steel makes the blade  of the weapon difficult to properly  sharpen and means it will always remain  somewhat delicate therefore clashing  against other blades or the armor of  your fellow man is not advised in the  witcher 3 at least this is reflected by  your silver sword doing less damage and  degrading more quickly when used against  brigands and bandits so while it's  established that a silver sword would in  no way make for a decent fighting weapon  it's interesting to note how the witcher  still tries to balance the pop culture  myths with a touch of reality even with  bronze swords early iron swords they  deform there are plenty of references in  roman literature in norse literature to  warriors swords bending out of shape and  in some cases you know they put it under  their foot they bend it back into the  shape and they keep on fighting but what  it shows you is that a soft blade even  one that is you know tough enough to  withstand a certain amount of of  battlefield rigor it gets bent out of  shape it becomes useless you won't  always have the time to bend it back  into shape so i think what we have to to  finish off by saying is yeah if this guy  is a zombie knight you know if he's not  made of flesh and bone and that's not  steel armor and he's just some kind of  manifestation then yeah i can probably  slice him i can stab him i can do what i  like with my silver sword but at the end  of the day  i think i'd still trust a good old steel  sword  so no unfortunately the silver sword is  not a feasible blade but it still holds  power as a unique and iconic weapon in  our pop culture the witch's own is an  armament that is held up by centuries of  folklore cinema and storytelling but  still succeeds in growing that myth and  creating a sword iconic to its own  stories as well as expanding the  profession of monster slayer within  wider pop culture whether from its use  as a healer and purifier in our real  history or as a weapon against beasts  and monsters in our fantasy silver is  anything but second best  [Music]  thank you so much for watching this  week's episode of loadout if you enjoyed  hit the like button and subscribe for  more just like this and of course let me  know your thoughts in either the comment  section down below or reach out to me on  twitter at a regular dave if you want  more video game weaponry content you can  watch our previous series of this show  or check out our weekly series of expert  reacts where i show our friends at the  royal armories a selection of video game  arms to break down and make sure to come  back next week for a deep dive into one  of the most iconic weapons of the second  world war the m1 garand  until next time i've been your host dave  dewitt and i will see you in the next  episode  [Music]  you

